URL: https://sourceforge.net/projects/pcre/files/pcre/8.39/pcre-8.39.zip/download
Version: 8.39
License: BSD
License File: files\LICENCE

Description:
This is version 8.39 of the PCRE regular expression matching library from
http://www.pcre.org/

Not 8.45, which is the last of the 8.x line: 8.39 is the newest release that
still builds and behaves as this tree expects without further work. Sawbuck
uses the C++ wrapper, pcrecpp::RE, from sawbuck/viewer/filter.cc.

Local Modifications:
Four generated headers, which upstream expects a configure run to produce and
which are checked in so this build needs no autotools:

  config.h             from config.h.generic, with twelve options enabled
  pcre.h               a straight copy of pcre.h.generic
  pcrecpparg.h         from pcrecpparg.h.in
  pcre_stringpiece.h   from pcre_stringpiece.h.in

The substitutions in the two .in files are pcre_have_long_long and
pcre_have_ulong_long set to 1, and pcre_have_type_traits and
pcre_have_bits_type_traits set to 0 -- the same choices the 8.00 copy made.

config.h enables SUPPORT_UTF, SUPPORT_PCRE8, HAVE_MEMMOVE, HAVE_STRERROR,
HAVE_STRING, HAVE_STRING_H, HAVE_LIMITS_H, HAVE_STDINT_H, HAVE_STDLIB_H,
HAVE_LONG_LONG, HAVE_UNSIGNED_LONG_LONG and HAVE__STRTOI64. Everything else
is config.h.generic's own value, including LINK_SIZE 2, MATCH_LIMIT 10000000,
MAX_NAME_COUNT 10000, MAX_NAME_SIZE 32, NEWLINE 10 and
POSIX_MALLOC_THRESHOLD 10, which is what the 8.00 config.h had as well.
PCRE_STATIC, LINK_SIZE and HAVE_CONFIG_H come from pcre.gyp's
target_defaults rather than from here.

Two things changed in the move from 8.00, both worth knowing before the next
upgrade:

  SUPPORT_UTF8 became SUPPORT_UTF plus SUPPORT_PCRE8 in PCRE 8.30.
  pcre_internal.h still back-translates the old name, but carries a "Fixme:
  SUPPORT_UTF8 should eventually disappear" beside it, so the new spelling is
  what is set here.

  HAVE_BCOPY was dropped. The 8.00 config.h asserted it, which was untrue on
  MSVC -- an artefact of that file having come from a Unix configure run. It
  never mattered, because HAVE_MEMMOVE is checked first everywhere PCRE uses
  either, but it was a lie. The other Unix-only claims in that file went with
  it: HAVE_BZLIB_H, HAVE_ZLIB_H, HAVE_READLINE_*, HAVE_DLFCN_H,
  HAVE_MEMORY_H, HAVE_STRINGS_H, HAVE_INTTYPES_H and LT_OBJDIR. Only pcregrep
  and pcretest would consult the compression and readline ones, and neither is
  built here.

And two edits to one upstream source. Both are warning fixes with no change in
behaviour, and neither has been taken upstream -- expect to re-apply them:

  pcrecpp.cc, NewlineMode's else branch: the always-failing assert was written
  'assert(NULL == "message")', which MSVC flags as C4130, a comparison against
  the address of a string constant. Rewritten as 'assert(false && "message")',
  the usual spelling of the same idiom. The assertion still always fires and
  still prints the message.

  pcrecpp.cc, RE::NumberOfCapturingGroups: pcre_fullinfo's result is read only
  by an assert, so a release build -- where assert compiles away -- leaves it
  unreferenced and MSVC reports C4189. A (void) cast after the assert says
  that is deliberate.

Not built, and not pruned from the vendored copy either, so that this stays a
whole upstream release rather than a subset nobody chose deliberately:

  pcre16_* and pcre32_*   the 16- and 32-bit libraries, which need
                          SUPPORT_PCRE16 or SUPPORT_PCRE32
  sljit/                  the JIT backend, reached only through
                          pcre_jit_compile.c, which is left out of pcre.gyp
                          because SUPPORT_JIT is not set
  pcre_jit_test.c         a test program for the above

pcre_printint.c is in the pcretest target rather than pcre_lib, which is where
upstream's Makefile.am puts it.
